#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130300} :activity/id #uuid "bf654963-a5d1-5562-85bc-b7c2fbbe6d40" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049265 :topic/id #uuid "e80c9bca-b203-5866-835e-d0d29bbfdf22" :topic/math-academy-id 1995} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130300 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130449 :tutorial/content "Recall that when we rotate a curve by $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis, the area generated by this surface of revolution is given by\n\n$$\nS =  \\int 2\\pi y \\, \\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nNow suppose we have a parametrically defined curve: \n\n$$\nx = x(t), \\qquad\ny = y(t), \\qquad\nt \\in \\left[t_1,t_2\\right].\n$$\n\nIn the case of a parametric curve, the arc length element $\\textrm{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{d}s = \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following formula for the surface area generated by rotating a parametrically defined curve about the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} y(t) \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "36a57433-1c78-58b8-860c-0ea6e3471761" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6113 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c5c27e9e-03cb-5a93-8a56-bd33588960fe" :step/math-academy-id 618749 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130301}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130301 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130466 :tutorial/content "Consider the curve defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx = \\cos{t}, \\qquad\ny = \\sin{t}, \\qquad\nt \\in [0,\\pi].\n$$\n\nIf we rotate this curve by $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis, we obtain a sphere of radius $1.$ \n\n![](images/d0/d0d323b764b70d3f2830d821636a9bc27b40e7f7e9f45df909c81dd279dc16e4.png)\n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area of our surface of revolution can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-2\\pi (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-2\\pi (-1-1) \\\\  & =4\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f849fe7f-670f-5a7f-8dc7-dbbd33a876f1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7707 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "031d5fb3-1834-595b-8a36-577430a0f9a7" :step/math-academy-id 618750 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130302}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130302 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129318 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129073 :question/id #uuid "066f76be-83f6-52fd-ab3f-00786d18f64e" :question/math-academy-id "e-9866"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b02082a8-b56e-5478-859d-51db7273f765" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050860}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129487} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129493} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388599} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435123} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527172}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of X"} :step/id #uuid "a6a3e1f3-c536-58d3-a81e-7966262884ce" :step/math-academy-id 618751 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130303}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130303 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130450 :tutorial/content "When we rotate a curve by $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis, the area generated by this surface of revolution is given by\n\n$$\nS =  \\int 2\\pi x \\, \\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in the case of a parametrically defined curve \n\n$$\nx = x(t), \\qquad\ny = y(t), \\qquad\nt \\in [t_1,t_2],\n$$\n\nthe formula for the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} x(t) \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm{d}t.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "548401c3-a52c-5444-b834-57c50c4bfa72" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6145 :tutorial/title "Surface Area Generated by Functions of Y"} :step/id #uuid "622390da-a9e6-55af-8449-1ceed5d4cec3" :step/math-academy-id 618754 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130304}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130304 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129319 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129074 :question/id #uuid "b7f404a3-8885-5a33-9cfb-1e12a6c745a8" :question/math-academy-id "e-9867"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a9a59d4-e8c8-59ac-bf23-eb70254a7661" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049491}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129488} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129491} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269201} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367477} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388592} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435124} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530457}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of Y"} :step/id #uuid "ddd7ced1-9fe0-533d-bd7a-9dfbcff663f8" :step/math-academy-id 618755 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130305}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130305 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129320 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129075 :question/id #uuid "205f4d6b-affa-530e-979b-a394e7a46bd3" :question/math-academy-id "e-9869"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7087df58-f97e-5423-813a-9aada16ee12a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129489} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319246} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320951} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368433} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368436} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435127} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435128} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530454} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530465} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530493}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Surface Area of Revolution"} :step/id #uuid "2f4e64a8-3397-5e7e-b805-080c59c56440" :step/math-academy-id 618758}] :activity/title "Surface Areas of Revolution for Parametric Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129340}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530558} :activity/id #uuid "f6065dfd-4199-50b0-9334-da4b9f494b0d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049265 :topic/id #uuid "e80c9bca-b203-5866-835e-d0d29bbfdf22" :topic/math-academy-id 1995} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530549 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530457 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6878ad2d-c3f6-5472-b932-d7a4364f9bc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡2{\\int }_{0}^{3}{4}^{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529841} {:answer/id #uuid "735f35d2-93c2-528a-acc9-d6a1cf48503f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+{4}^{t}{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529872} {:answer/id #uuid "8f18c374-0ec5-5b5a-8aa2-bf73f6a415be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡2{\\int }_{0}^{3}{2}^{t}\\sqrt{{2}^{t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529926} {:answer/id #uuid "b4866bd0-791b-5423-befb-168b1ad8cf39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{2}^{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+{2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530030} {:answer/id #uuid "cb3c2df3-9d88-53e0-973d-199d14e0ed36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡2{\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{4}{2}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530090}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529872} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4a256d7-74ef-5f59-aa17-d233ba137369" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530348}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5349d9de-8c6d-5416-a022-6878977b6004" :question/math-academy-id "q-139057" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={t}^{2},\\,y={2}^{t},\\,t\\in [0,3].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=3.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})=2t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({2}^{t})={2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{{(2t)}^{2}+{({2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+{2}^{2t}{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+{4}^{t}{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "032c215a-e883-53c2-bdc0-c14d20b9401e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530563}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530550 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530454 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3256b67-7e99-5826-a95a-b55a197e4d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530184}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530184} :answer-field/id #uuid "12fa28de-d131-50b4-b0e1-7dd696b0e54f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530230}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4f72f786-eb31-5b03-9051-ae70858e9f5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-352035" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={t}^{2},\\,y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}{t}^{4},\\,t\\in [0,\\sqrt[4]{6}].\n$$\n\nDetermine the surface area $S$ generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\sqrt[4]{6}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})=2t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}{t}^{4})=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}}{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+{(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}}{t}^{3})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+\\frac{16}{3}{t}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}(1+\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{4})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}{t}^{2}\\cdot 2|t|\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{4}}\\cdot {t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=1+\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{4}.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=\\frac{16}{3}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t⟹{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{3}{16}\\text{d}u.$ We also update the limits of integration for $u:$\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $\\sqrt[4]{6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $9$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{4}}\\cdot {t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{3}{16}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{9}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\cdot {[\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}[(9)^{3/2}-(1)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}(27-1) \\\\  & =13\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "08362200-3b6f-52bb-9fff-49cbac1aba34" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530555}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530555 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530493 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c15a0040-721e-5583-80c4-6b4b6d161036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi(2-\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530062}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530062} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b596d95-5abf-5a25-8619-bd46ab687c7e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530300}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "95891d65-20e2-5732-9bd4-e52bbf49b5d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-352088" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})),\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nDetermine the surface area $S$ generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ to ${t}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}))) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t/2)}\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{csc}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{csc}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{csc}^{2}⁡t-2+{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{csc}^{2}⁡t-2+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{csc}^{2}⁡t-1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{cot}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.$ We also update the limits of integration for $u:$\n\n| **$t$** | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/2}^{1}2\\pi \\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\pi {[u]}_{\\sqrt{3}/2}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\pi (1-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\pi (2-\\sqrt{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3a2cc474-bbd9-524c-88cd-2cfdbe4a121b"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530558 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129488 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "276f10df-f807-54a9-b01f-7cf1b357ec66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529665} {:answer/id #uuid "8f170c4b-c5f7-5356-8a5a-6fd851ea13ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529925} {:answer/id #uuid "a53f81c5-8ba3-5b6b-9818-2872dc69946f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529990} {:answer/id #uuid "b86bdc8d-5bf9-5ea7-8414-b3dab58e55bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530042} {:answer/id #uuid "e9f6ac1e-5d34-5fa1-a3ce-c20f5afa43d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530163}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529990} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a8fb9e3-9863-5703-95c1-0d8295fc07d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "497a1af7-923f-5a2e-82d5-147a5d2a1d6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-138945" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=3t,\\,y=4t,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)=3 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}3t\\sqrt{{(3)}^{2}+{(4)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}3t\\sqrt{25}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}3t\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =30\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "50c04e39-0863-56d5-a574-6b3bc23fdf01" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530549}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530563 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530465 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "961eb0d5-8253-50e6-a961-755f2db89486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529944}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529944} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3f06b1d-08c6-50bc-8914-e903872c2d7d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6528b016-7e7a-5f42-bb80-0fc7063586be" :question/math-academy-id "q-352031" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,y=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,t\\in [0,\\sqrt{3}].\n$$\n\nDetermine the surface area $S$ generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\sqrt{3}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{2}t)=\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2})=\\frac{1}{2}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{2}t\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2}t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{2}t\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{2}t\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}(1+{t}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{2}t\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\cdot 2t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=1+{t}^{2}.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=2t\\,\\text{d}t.$ We also update the limits of integration for $u:$\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\cdot 2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot {[\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}[(4)^{3/2}-(1)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}(8-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8d943a17-8038-5310-ba12-619cf9e0f21c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530550}}] :activity/title "Surface Areas of Revolution for Parametric Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529573}]}
